Write a self-contained prompt that would let an agent recreate this script.
Output only the prompt.
Write a single-file Python 3 command-line script (intended to run under Termux
on Android, so use the `#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`
shebang) that extracts plain text from PDF files, one page at a time, using a
selectable backend and optional parallel processing.
Purpose and behavior: - Accept one or more PDF file paths (and/or directories,
if reasonable) on the command line and produce extracted text for every page,
writing results to stdout or to output files. - Support multiple extraction
backends, selected by a command-line option: 1. pdfplumber backend: open the PDF
with pdfplumber, iterate over the requested page indices, and call the page's
text extraction method, treating a None result as an empty string. 2.
PyMuPDF (fitz) backend: open the PDF with fitz, iterate over page indices, and
call get_text.
When an option "sort" is enabled, call get_text with the "text" mode and
sort=True so text reads in visual order; otherwise call get_text with default
arguments.
Close the document in a finally block. 3.
Additional backend(s) for other PDF libraries may be included in the same
pattern; the design must make it easy to add backends through a dispatch table
or mapping from backend name to extraction function. - Each extraction worker
function has the signature (pdf_path: str, page_indices: Sequence[int], opts:
dict) and returns a list of (1-based page number, text) tuples.
Import the backend library inside the worker function, not at module top level,
so that a missing library only fails when that backend is actually used. -
Per-page errors must not abort the run: catch exceptions per page, print a
message to stderr in the form "Error extracting page N from <path>:
<exception>", and substitute an empty string for that page's text. - Use the
standard library only for the CLI itself: argparse for arguments, pathlib for
paths, sys for stderr/stdout, concurrent.futures for parallelism
(ProcessPoolExecutor for CPU-bound backends or a ThreadPoolExecutor for
I/O-bound use; choose via an option or a sensible default, and document the
choice in code comments only if needed). - Parallelism: split a document's page
indices into chunks and dispatch chunks to the executor with as_completed, then
reassemble results sorted by page number so output order is deterministic
regardless of completion order.
Provide a command-line option for the number of workers and a default (for
example, the CPU count capped at a small number).
A worker count of 1 should run without an executor. - Page selection: support a
page range or list option (for example "1-5,8,10-") with 1-based inputs
converted to 0-based indices, clamped or validated against the document's page
count, and reported with a clear error for invalid specifications. - Output
options: write to stdout by default, or to an output file path; optionally write
one text file per input PDF into an output directory.
Separate pages with a clear, consistent page marker (for example a form-feed or
a line such as "--- Page N ---") and ensure output is UTF-8 encoded regardless
of the terminal locale. - Sorting option: a --sort flag that is forwarded in
opts to the PyMuPDF backend. - Error handling: validate that each input path
exists and is a PDF; on a missing file or unreadable PDF, print an error to
stderr, skip that file, continue with the rest, and exit with a non-zero status
at the end if any file failed.
Handle KeyboardInterrupt by cancelling pending futures and exiting with status
130.
Return integer exit codes from a main() function and call it under the standard
`if __name__ == "__main__":` guard using sys.exit. - If a required backend
library is not installed, print a helpful message to stderr naming the package
to install (pdfplumber or PyMuPDF) and exit non-zero rather than crashing with a
traceback. - Include a module docstring or top-of-file comment describing usage
with examples, and use type hints throughout (with `from __future__ import
annotations`).
Edge cases to handle: - Empty PDFs or pages with no extractable text (produce
empty content, not errors). - Zero pages selected after range filtering (warn
and skip the file). - Duplicate or overlapping page ranges (deduplicate and keep
sorted order). - Paths with spaces or non-ASCII characters. - Very large
documents (chunking keeps memory bounded; avoid loading all text before writing
if output is streamed). - Worker count larger than page count (cap it).
Output only the final Python script as the deliverable, complete and runnable,
with a short argparse help text for each option. ---
